 run, but did not show a clear overall speed gain. It remains disabled in the
 private game configuration. Adequate Dolphin gameplay speed is still open.
+
+## GIF arbiter single-packet fast path (unreleased, always on)
+
+`PS2Memory::submitGifPacket` with immediate drain now calls `GifArbiter::submitAndDrain`. With an empty queue, no re-entrancy and a packet of at most 4 KiB it snapshots the packet into a reused scratch buffer and processes it directly, skipping the per-packet heap vector, queue push, `stable_sort` temporary buffer and free. Larger packets (image uploads), a pending queue or a re-entrant call take the previous submit+drain route, and `drain()` no longer sorts a single entry. Host test `gs_gif_arbiter` compares random packets (all paths, DIRECTHL/IMAGE flags, pending-queue, re-entrant and above-limit cases) byte- and order-identical to the old route.
+
+Lesson learned: the first version had no size limit, so the scratch buffer grew to the largest packet and stayed allocated. Dolphin runs of that Wii build ended with `std::bad_alloc` within ~110 s (2 of 2 runs), while the old build ran on. Memory headroom on Wii is tiny; never keep large buffers alive for a micro-optimization. With the 4 KiB bound, four paired 150 s Dolphin runs (2 old, 2 new) all completed.
+
+Measured effect: host micro-timing about 70-107 ms -> 19 ms for 2 M 64-byte packets, but in the Dolphin startup window (first 150 s) the sampled GIF-submit phase stays 7.4 % in both builds and draw/phase progress is within run-to-run noise. No Wii or Dolphin speedup is established. The startup window is dominated by guest EE code (about 67 % of phase samples), not by GS work.
