--- orig/PS2Recomp/docs/wii/REBUILD-FROM-HANDOFF.md	1970-01-01 00:00:00.000000000 +0000
+++ src/PS2Recomp/docs/wii/REBUILD-FROM-HANDOFF.md	2026-10-30 09:09:24.049819749 +0000
@@ -0,0 +1,24 @@
+# Rebuilding the private Crash Wii build from the handoff archives
+
+The handoff archives contain no generated guest code and no VU AOT registry. They can be recreated from the owner ELF (private, never commit the outputs):
+
+1. Recompiler: build `ps2xRecomp`/`ps2xAnalyzer` from the repository root (`-DPS2X_BUILD_RUNTIME=OFF -DPS2X_BUILD_TEST=OFF -DPS2X_BUILD_STUDIO=OFF`, plus `-DCMAKE_CXX_FLAGS=-I<repo>/ps2xIOP/include` for `ps2x/guest_endian.h`). Run `ps2_analyzer game.elf config.toml`, then `ps2_recomp config.toml`. For the Crash ELF this produced 2,852 files whose 2,849 function names match the function symbols of the earlier private Wii ELF exactly.
+2. VU AOT registry: the earlier ELF carries the registry words as template arguments (`VU1AotRegistry::runUpper<N>` / `runLower<N>`). Extracting them gives 559 upper / 944 lower words, the counts recorded in the handoff notes. A profile with `specialize_flags` and `predecode_usage` fed to `tools/vu/generate_aot.py` regenerates a words-only registry. Program-block lists (`programs[]`) are not recoverable this way, so blocks are absent: this rebuild is not identical to the earlier DOL.
+3. Wii target: configure `ps2xRuntime/platform/wii` with `PS2X_GENERATED_DIR`, `PS2X_VU_AOT_DIR`, `PS2X_GEKKO_ROOT` and the options of the last private build as far as known (NATIVE_GX, SAMPLE_PROFILE, VU lazy decode / active pipelines / XGKICK spans / AOT usage / normal flags fast path / packet reuse, VU fast cache, HOT_O2, source image cache, immutable source tiles, GX depth24 / constant stages / batch linear runs / fixed run vertices / profile families, GS triangle row spans and upload row cursor). Use the system `cmake`/`python3`, not the relocated virtualenv launchers. A full build is 2,940 translation units (about 15 minutes with four jobs) and passed the static layout check (MEM1 SDK region and 4 MiB headroom assertion).
+4. Dolphin needs Debian 13 userland libraries (ffmpeg 7, Qt 6, libzip 5); a `debootstrap trixie` chroot works. Prepare the SD image with `tools/wii/prepare_disc.py` plus `game.elf` (FAT, `ps2recomp/` directory).
+
+Observation from the 150 s startup window of this rebuild: first GX draws appear after about 90 s of host time, guest EE code is about 67 % of phase samples and GIF submission 7 %. No resident Z24 submission occurred. These are Dolphin wall-time progress numbers, not Wii FPS.
+
+## Boot-time finding and IOP interpreter batch (2026-10-30)
+
+A 100 s guest-PC / PowerPC-PC sampling of the rebuilt Dolphin run showed that the EE sits in `sceSifCallRpc` (73 % of samples; 92-93 % between 20 and 80 s) while the host executes the IOP interpreter: `endian::read<uint32_t>` 21.5 %, `IopCpuCore::executeInstruction` 19 %, `IopEmulator::Impl::step` 19 %, `memset` 9 %, `runCpu` 7 %, `schedulePendingDma` 6 %. The slow image build-up at start is IOP module initialisation, not GS or VU work.
+
+Batch 1 (semantics-preserving): `ps2x::endian::read/write` use a single byte-reversed load/store (`__builtin_bswap*`, always inline) on big-endian hosts instead of a byte loop; `schedulePendingDma` returns early through a cheap `dmaStartPending()` test; new option `PS2X_WII_IOP_O2` compiles the IOP interpreter sources with `-O2` instead of `-Os`. Dolphin A/B (host wall time, time until the EE leaves the RPC wait): old build 86-88 s (2 runs), new build 50-55 s (2 runs), about 40 % shorter. This is Dolphin host time, not Wii time, and only the boot phase.
+
+## Batch 2: IOP profile and audio-RPC bypass (2026-10-30, owner-private diagnostic)
+
+New diagnostic option `PS2X_WII_IOP_PC_PROFILE` (default OFF): `PS2IopPcHist` (1-in-16 IOP PC histogram, 16-byte buckets), `PS2IopInstrTotal`, a one-shot 64 KiB IOP RAM dump (`PS2IopRamDump`, from 0x3E000 after 60 M instructions) and `PS2IopRpcStats[3][64]` (calls / IOP instructions / sid per RPC function). Read through `/proc/<dolphin>/mem` like the existing profiler.
+
+Findings for the Crash boot (Dolphin): about 199 M IOP instructions in 65 s; the histogram is identical across windows, i.e. a steady loop. In 45 s one RPC accounted for essentially all IOP work: SID `0x12345`, function 0, 19,323 calls, 162 M instructions (about 8,400 per call, roughly 430 calls/s). The hot code is an unoptimised (-O0) IOP sound module that polls SPU2 state per voice through `LIBSD.IRX` imports. The EE side is the game's per-iteration `SoundUpdate` -> `FlushIOPCommand` -> `sceSifCallRpc`. Because emulated time advances with every call, boot time was dominated by interpreting this audio command port.
+
+Option `PS2X_IOP_NULL_RPC_SID=<sid>` (cache string, default empty, owner-private): `IopSubsystem::handleRpc` completes that SID at once without running the IOP; the receive buffer is zeroed, nothing else is invented. Audio output is disabled in this runtime anyway. With `0x12345` (Dolphin, wall time, same boot, `CPUThread=False`): first GX draws before 20 s (C1 build: ~55 s, B2 build: ~90 s); the GX attempt counter at 60 s equals the old build at ~2-2.5 minutes; the 40 s frame has the same hash as an earlier frame of the unmodified build; the run continued for 300 s without exception. Not verified: that no later game state needs audio RPC results, any audio behaviour, and any physical Wii timing. Steady-state rendering speed after boot is unchanged (phase shares at 300 s: CPU GS raster 22 %, GIF submit 18 %, VU1 18 %, guest 16 %).
